Hi Karl,
I was trying to figure out how to speed up 'droponemarker' and it looks like it does't look for the 'n.cluster' for est.map to parallelize the map estimation. Is it possible that this could speed it up? Or maybe this is already addressed in qtl2?
Jeff

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Wed Aug 29 18:58:48 2018
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Wed, 29 Aug 2018 18:58:48 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] Droponemarker calls est.map without 'n.cluster'
Message-ID: <ps5WGLz5B7I.KAXCQxGAAAAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <ps5WGLz5B7I.-14PbuUdAgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <ps5WGLz5B7I.-14PbuUdAgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/ps5WGLz5B7I/m/KAXCQxGAAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The n.cluster argument in est.map() parallelizes on the chromosome level. Since droponemarker() considers a single chromosome, it wouldn't really help. What's needed would be to parallelize on the marker, in this line: https://github.com/kbroman/qtl/blob/master/R/droponemarker.R#L80

I think the main way to speed it up is through by using a small number for maxit; you probably don't need to get a great map estimate in order to identify problem markers.

We've not yet implemented this in R/qtl2.

karl

